1
00:00:31,668 --> 00:00:34,230
Away from all land

2
00:00:35,513 --> 00:00:36,793
The ocean

3
00:00:38,396 --> 00:00:41,880
It covers more
than half the surface of our planet

4
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:42,721
And yet

5
00:00:42,761 --> 00:00:44,203
for the most part

6
00:00:44,243 --> 00:00:46,645
it is
beyond our reach

7
00:00:50,370 --> 00:00:53,372
Much of it is
virtually empty

8
00:00:54,574 --> 00:00:56,496
A watery desert

9
00:01:02,397 --> 00:01:04,519
All life
that is here

10
00:01:04,559 --> 00:01:08,722
is locked in a
constant search to find food

11
00:01:10,243 --> 00:01:13,806
A struggle to
conserve precious energy

12
00:01:13,846 --> 00:01:16,087
in the open ocean

13
00:01:36,266 --> 00:01:40,111
The biggest
of all fish

14
00:01:40,151 --> 00:01:42,034
30 tons in weight

15
00:01:42,074 --> 00:01:43,876
12 metres long

16
00:01:44,278 --> 00:01:46,320
The whale shark

17
00:01:47,843 --> 00:01:53,931
Its huge bulk is
sustained by near microscopic creatures of the sea

18
00:01:54,933 --> 00:01:56,134
Plankton

19
00:01:58,423 --> 00:02:02,788
Whale sharks
cruise on regular habitual routes

20
00:02:02,828 --> 00:02:05,590
between the
best feeding grounds

21
00:02:09,956 --> 00:02:11,318
In February

22
00:02:11,358 --> 00:02:13,881
that takes them
to the surface waters

23
00:02:13,921 --> 00:02:16,724
far from the
coast of Venezuela

24
00:02:20,089 --> 00:02:22,571
Others are
already here

25
00:02:23,195 --> 00:02:26,197
Bait fish have
come for the same reason

26
00:02:26,278 --> 00:02:28,239
to feed on
the plankton

27
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:33,484
The whale shark
has timed its arrival exactly right

28
00:02:35,167 --> 00:02:36,048
Oddly

29
00:02:36,088 --> 00:02:39,050
the tiny fish
swarm around it

30
00:02:47,242 --> 00:02:49,925
They're using
it as a shield

31
00:02:53,772 --> 00:02:57,577
Other predatory
fish are lurking nearby

32
00:03:00,623 --> 00:03:02,425
Yellowfin tuna

33
00:03:02,986 --> 00:03:05,750
They seem
wary of the giant

34
00:03:12,318 --> 00:03:13,882
The shark dives

35
00:03:13,922 --> 00:03:17,367
as if to escape
from such overcrowding

36
00:03:22,218 --> 00:03:24,342
Now the tuna
have a chance

37
00:03:24,382 --> 00:03:27,668
to attack the
unprotected baitfish

38
00:03:39,525 --> 00:03:40,605
But then

39
00:03:40,887 --> 00:03:43,209
back comes
the giant

40
00:03:50,202 --> 00:03:54,043
It has taken a
vast mouthful of the baitfish itself

41
00:03:54,363 --> 00:03:55,924
Plankton it seems

42
00:03:55,964 --> 00:03:58,204
is not the
only food

43
00:03:58,244 --> 00:03:59,925
for a whale shark

44
00:04:10,648 --> 00:04:14,408
Both shark and
tuna feast together

45
00:04:30,136 --> 00:04:32,617
But the tuna
must beware

46
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:41,062
Even they can end
up in the whale shark's stomach

47
00:04:49,349 --> 00:04:53,874
Predators here
must grab what they can when they can

48
00:04:53,995 --> 00:04:56,758
For such events
do not last long

49
00:04:59,890 --> 00:05:02,931
The dense shoals
on which so many depend

50
00:05:02,971 --> 00:05:06,453
gather only when
water conditions are perfect

51
00:05:20,820 --> 00:05:25,702
Many predators spend much of their time cruising the open ocean

52
00:05:25,782 --> 00:05:27,623
endlessly
searching

53
00:05:32,675 --> 00:05:35,195
Plankton
feeding rays do so

54
00:05:35,275 --> 00:05:38,075
gliding with
minimum effort

55
00:05:52,991 --> 00:05:55,911
The oceanic
white tip shark

56
00:05:58,351 --> 00:06:01,190
Another energy
efficient traveller

57
00:06:03,830 --> 00:06:09,630
It specialises in
locating prey in the emptiest areas of the open ocean

58
00:06:09,670 --> 00:06:12,949
Patrolling the
top 100 metres of water

59
00:06:21,881 --> 00:06:26,526
Taste in water is
the equivalent of smell in the air

60
00:06:27,008 --> 00:06:32,133
An oceanic white
tip is able to detect even the faintest trace

61
00:06:36,139 --> 00:06:38,622
Small pilot
fish swim with it

62
00:06:38,662 --> 00:06:42,265
The shark can
find prey far more easily than they can

63
00:06:42,306 --> 00:06:45,669
and they'll be
able to collect the scraps from its meals

64
00:06:51,007 --> 00:06:54,287
Its long fixed
pectoral fins

65
00:06:54,327 --> 00:06:56,927
enable it to
soar through the water

66
00:06:56,967 --> 00:06:59,967
with the least
expenditure of energy

67
00:07:05,047 --> 00:07:09,447
This shark has
found a school of rainbow runners

68
00:07:10,127 --> 00:07:12,647
It would eat one
given the chance

69
00:07:12,967 --> 00:07:15,367
But rainbow
runners are swift and agile

70
00:07:15,407 --> 00:07:17,247
and not
easily caught

71
00:07:20,752 --> 00:07:23,154
So it
bides its time

72
00:07:23,395 --> 00:07:25,557
There's a chance
that eventually

73
00:07:25,597 --> 00:07:27,719
it may spot a
weakened fish

74
00:07:27,759 --> 00:07:29,320
that's catchable

75
00:07:33,085 --> 00:07:34,326
The hunter

76
00:07:34,647 --> 00:07:36,528
endlessly waiting

77
00:07:47,941 --> 00:07:50,584
Excitement
far from land

78
00:07:59,879 --> 00:08:02,481
A school
of dolphin

79
00:08:02,521 --> 00:08:03,642
500 strong

80
00:08:19,497 --> 00:08:21,860
They've sensed
there's food around

81
00:08:21,900 --> 00:08:24,422
And they're
racing to catch up with it

82
00:08:32,716 --> 00:08:34,318
The news
has spread

83
00:08:34,358 --> 00:08:37,722
now a number of
schools are on their way

84
00:08:51,743 --> 00:08:54,344
They're heading
towards the Azores

85
00:08:54,384 --> 00:08:58,545
volcanic islands
a thousand miles west of Portugal

86
00:09:09,606 --> 00:09:12,892
The dolphin scan
the water ahead with their sonar

87
00:09:12,932 --> 00:09:15,095
They're close
to their target

88
00:09:23,871 --> 00:09:25,393
This is it

89
00:09:25,635 --> 00:09:27,477
Scad mackerel

90
00:09:30,809 --> 00:09:34,889
It's difficult
for a single dolphin to catch the fish

91
00:09:36,249 --> 00:09:37,929
To avoid
wasting energy

92
00:09:37,969 --> 00:09:39,889
they work
as a group

93
00:09:47,208 --> 00:09:49,048
They drive
the fish upwards

94
00:09:49,088 --> 00:09:51,688
trapping them
against the surface

95
00:09:53,888 --> 00:09:54,888
And there

96
00:09:54,928 --> 00:09:57,248
other predators await them

97
00:10:00,804 --> 00:10:02,925
Cory shearwaters

98
00:10:03,566 --> 00:10:05,247
They're waiting
for the dolphin

99
00:10:05,287 --> 00:10:08,529
to drive the prey
closer to the surface

100
00:10:18,816 --> 00:10:21,978
Now the shearwaters can dive down on them

101
00:10:22,018 --> 00:10:25,059
descending to
20 metres or more

102
00:10:29,330 --> 00:10:32,975
And the dolphins
block the bait balls retreat

103
00:11:04,461 --> 00:11:08,345
The dolphins leave as soon as they've had their fill

104
00:11:13,111 --> 00:11:18,998
And at last the mackerel sink below the diving range of the birds

105
00:11:43,096 --> 00:11:45,177
As the sun
disappears

106
00:11:45,217 --> 00:11:48,858
a profound change
takes place in the ocean

107
00:11:50,262 --> 00:11:53,946
Deep water plankton start to rise from the depths

108
00:11:53,986 --> 00:11:58,550
And another
hungry army prepares to receive it

109
00:12:23,510 --> 00:12:24,712
Every night

110
00:12:24,752 --> 00:12:26,754
wherever
conditions are right

111
00:12:26,794 --> 00:12:29,798
countless millions of creatures from the deep

112
00:12:29,838 --> 00:12:31,720
migrate to
the surface

113
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:33,322
seeking food

114
00:12:42,718 --> 00:12:44,560
A baby sailfish

115
00:12:45,002 --> 00:12:46,884
15cm long

116
00:12:46,924 --> 00:12:49,367
snaps up
everything in its path

117
00:12:50,048 --> 00:12:51,610
In three
years time

118
00:12:51,650 --> 00:12:54,935
it'll be one of
the ocean's most formidable hunters

119
00:12:54,975 --> 00:12:57,137
weighing 60 kilos

120
00:12:58,219 --> 00:12:59,782
Just now，however

121
00:12:59,822 --> 00:13:02,024
it's very
vulnerable

122
00:13:44,607 --> 00:13:48,048
These manta
rays are giants

123
00:13:49,049 --> 00:13:52,651
8 metres across
and weighing over two tons

124
00:13:56,414 --> 00:13:59,696
The bladelike
projections on either side of the head

125
00:13:59,736 --> 00:14:03,377
help to steer
plankton into the manta's mouth

126
00:14:23,566 --> 00:14:25,367
Dawn returns

127
00:14:25,407 --> 00:14:28,528
and the plankton
sinks back into the depths

128
00:14:30,810 --> 00:14:32,491
If we are
to follow

129
00:14:32,531 --> 00:14:35,052
we must use
a submarine

130
00:14:38,501 --> 00:14:41,141
As we descend
into the darkness

131
00:14:41,181 --> 00:14:42,980
The pressure builds

132
00:14:43,020 --> 00:14:44,860
The temperature falls

133
00:14:49,019 --> 00:14:50,899
Below 500 metres

134
00:14:50,939 --> 00:14:53,739
new mysterious
animals appear

135
00:14:59,578 --> 00:15:01,378
Their
bizarre shapes

136
00:15:01,418 --> 00:15:03,618
help them to
remain suspended

137
00:15:03,658 --> 00:15:05,497
In the dark space

138
00:15:09,292 --> 00:15:14,296
Some resemble
creatures familiar from shallower waters

139
00:15:22,383 --> 00:15:25,666
Others defy
classification

140
00:15:31,471 --> 00:15:32,872
All around

141
00:15:32,912 --> 00:15:36,595
organic particles drift downwards

142
00:15:38,223 --> 00:15:39,704
Marine snow

143
00:15:39,744 --> 00:15:41,625
detritus from
the creatures

144
00:15:41,665 --> 00:15:44,748
swarming in the
sunlit waters above

145
00:15:47,030 --> 00:15:50,273
The snow is food
for many animals here

146
00:15:50,313 --> 00:15:52,434
like the
sea spider

147
00:15:52,555 --> 00:15:55,677
a small relative
of shrimps and crabs

148
00:16:07,885 --> 00:16:11,287
Those strange
leglike appendages

149
00:16:11,327 --> 00:16:14,328
are feathered to
stop it from sinking

150
00:16:16,650 --> 00:16:19,931
They can also
enmesh marine snow

151
00:16:20,092 --> 00:16:23,653
which it wipes
carefully into its jaws

152
00:16:39,822 --> 00:16:45,067
A sawtooth eel
hangs upright and motionless

153
00:16:48,151 --> 00:16:49,913
Gazing
ever upwards

154
00:16:49,953 --> 00:16:54,517
it watches for prey silhouetted against the faint glimmerings of light

155
00:16:54,557 --> 00:16:56,038
from the surface

156
00:17:02,875 --> 00:17:04,638
Days may pass

157
00:17:04,680 --> 00:17:08,530
Before prey swims
close enough for it to strike

158
00:17:13,689 --> 00:17:15,332
Farther down still

159
00:17:15,372 --> 00:17:17,895
the blackness is complete

160
00:17:18,697 --> 00:17:20,821
No vestige
of sunlight

161
00:17:20,861 --> 00:17:23,504
can penetrate
as far as this

162
00:17:27,232 --> 00:17:29,875
Food is
very scarce

163
00:17:30,036 --> 00:17:33,561
And nothing can
afford to waste any energy

164
00:17:36,449 --> 00:17:40,293
A Dumbo octopus
simply flaps a fin

165
00:17:40,734 --> 00:17:42,937
No need for the
jet propulsion

166
00:17:42,977 --> 00:17:46,100
used by its
shallow water relatives above

167
00:18:17,393 --> 00:18:21,114
The weirdest in
this world of the strange

168
00:18:21,435 --> 00:18:23,476
Vampyrotothys

169
00:18:23,556 --> 00:18:26,757
the vampire
squid from hell

170
00:18:31,801 --> 00:18:33,121
Disturb it

171
00:18:33,321 --> 00:18:36,483
and it only
retreats a little distance

172
00:18:40,005 --> 00:18:41,446
Go after it

173
00:18:41,486 --> 00:18:43,927
and it has a
special defence

174
00:18:48,188 --> 00:18:49,748
To see
what it does

175
00:18:49,788 --> 00:18:51,788
you must
switch off the lights

176
00:18:54,867 --> 00:18:58,266
The vampire squid
has lights of its own

177
00:19:01,306 --> 00:19:05,945
Bioluminescent
bacteria shine from pockets on its arms

178
00:19:05,985 --> 00:19:08,104
To confuse
its predators

179
00:19:09,344 --> 00:19:10,744
Are those eyes？

180
00:19:15,775 --> 00:19:16,175
In fact

181
00:19:16,215 --> 00:19:18,377
there's spots at
the end of its mantle

182
00:19:18,417 --> 00:19:22,180
A bite there
would leave the head unscathed

183
00:19:24,422 --> 00:19:26,543
The threat
diminishes

184
00:19:26,704 --> 00:19:30,868
and Vampiro Toothys disappears into the blackness

185
00:19:47,784 --> 00:19:48,786
At last

186
00:19:48,826 --> 00:19:50,267
the sea floor

187
00:19:50,468 --> 00:19:52,911
Over two
miles down

188
00:19:53,312 --> 00:19:57,597
The pressure here
is 300 times that at the surface

189
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:00,643
It takes
several months

190
00:20:00,683 --> 00:20:04,688
for marine snow
to drift down as far as this

191
00:20:08,986 --> 00:20:13,265
As you travel away from the rocky margins of the continents

192
00:20:13,305 --> 00:20:16,585
An immense plain
stretches ahead

193
00:20:19,624 --> 00:20:22,624
It extends for
thousands of miles

194
00:20:22,904 --> 00:20:25,344
Gradually
sinking downwards

195
00:20:32,482 --> 00:20:35,523
There are faint
trails in the ooze

196
00:20:35,883 --> 00:20:38,084
Signs
that even here

197
00:20:38,124 --> 00:20:39,524
there is life

198
00:20:46,767 --> 00:20:49,928
These are what
made some of them

199
00:20:50,088 --> 00:20:54,809
Sea urchins
sifting the accumulating drifts

200
00:20:59,172 --> 00:21:02,451
Shrimps standing
on elegant tiptoe

201
00:21:02,491 --> 00:21:06,331
fastidiously select the particles that appeal to them

202
00:21:14,489 --> 00:21:16,290
But in
the deep sea

203
00:21:16,331 --> 00:21:18,131
as everywhere else

204
00:21:18,172 --> 00:21:19,893
if there
are grazers

205
00:21:20,093 --> 00:21:21,574
there are hunters

206
00:21:31,301 --> 00:21:32,822
A monkfish

207
00:21:33,062 --> 00:21:35,944
almost indistinguishable from the sand

208
00:21:35,984 --> 00:21:37,505
on which it lies

209
00:21:42,429 --> 00:21:44,672
Why waste
energy chasing around

210
00:21:44,712 --> 00:21:47,315
if you can
attract prey towards you

211
00:21:47,355 --> 00:21:48,756
with a lure？

212
00:22:07,872 --> 00:22:10,793
Maybe that one
was a bit big

213
00:22:13,675 --> 00:22:17,437
The monkfish can
wait for days if necessary

214
00:22:17,477 --> 00:22:20,838
until the
rightsized meal turns up

215
00:22:24,360 --> 00:22:26,361
Scavengers on
the other hand

216
00:22:26,401 --> 00:22:29,362
have to move
around to find their food

217
00:22:32,339 --> 00:22:35,898
Crabs can detect
the faintest of tastes in the water

218
00:22:35,938 --> 00:22:38,698
And that helps
them locate the latest body

219
00:22:38,738 --> 00:22:41,257
To drift
down from above

220
00:22:42,737 --> 00:22:45,976
Eels are already
feeding on the corpse

221
00:22:53,214 --> 00:22:54,254
Isopods

222
00:22:54,294 --> 00:22:57,853
like giant marine
woodlice a third of a meter long

223
00:22:57,893 --> 00:23:00,413
are ripping into
the rotting flesh

224
00:23:02,013 --> 00:23:03,532
Over the
next few hours

225
00:23:03,572 --> 00:23:05,412
there'll be
frenzied competition

226
00:23:05,452 --> 00:23:07,731
between
scavengers of all kinds

227
00:23:07,771 --> 00:23:09,331
to grab a share

228
00:23:39,664 --> 00:23:41,186
Just occasionally

229
00:23:41,226 --> 00:23:44,389
there is a
gigantic bonanza

230
00:23:53,682 --> 00:23:56,765
The remains of
a sperm whale

231
00:23:57,366 --> 00:24:00,249
He died five
months or so ago

232
00:24:00,570 --> 00:24:03,174
There's little
left but fatty blubber

233
00:24:03,214 --> 00:24:05,296
clinging
to its bones

234
00:24:08,375 --> 00:24:12,138
Its flesh has
nourished life for miles around

235
00:24:12,178 --> 00:24:15,179
But now the
feast is almost over

236
00:24:20,224 --> 00:24:22,746
Spider crabs
a metre across

237
00:24:22,786 --> 00:24:26,508
still pick at the
last putrid remains

238
00:24:34,232 --> 00:24:35,834
A few weeks more

239
00:24:35,874 --> 00:24:39,557
and nothing will
be left but bare bones

240
00:24:40,399 --> 00:24:42,521
The crabs
will have to fast

241
00:24:42,561 --> 00:24:45,884
until the next
carcass drifts down

242
00:24:52,572 --> 00:24:57,216
But not all food
comes from the sunlit world above

243
00:24:58,369 --> 00:25:00,772
The floor of
the Atlantic Ocean

244
00:25:00,812 --> 00:25:02,254
is split in two

245
00:25:02,294 --> 00:25:05,217
by an immense
volcanic mountain chain

246
00:25:05,257 --> 00:25:10,703
That winds unbroken for 45，000 miles around the globe

247
00:25:14,668 --> 00:25:15,709
In places

248
00:25:15,749 --> 00:25:18,312
it's riven by
great fissures

249
00:25:18,352 --> 00:25:20,635
From which
superheated water

250
00:25:20,675 --> 00:25:23,038
loaded with
dissolved minerals

251
00:25:23,078 --> 00:25:25,960
blasts into
the icy depths

252
00:25:37,118 --> 00:25:41,680
Clouds of sulphides solidify into towering chimneys

253
00:25:41,720 --> 00:25:44,481
as tall as a
threestorey house

254
00:25:49,164 --> 00:25:50,684
At 400 degrees

255
00:25:50,724 --> 00:25:53,446
this scalding
cocktail of chemicals

256
00:25:53,486 --> 00:25:57,287
would be lethally
toxic to most forms of life

257
00:25:59,493 --> 00:26:00,975
But astoundingly

258
00:26:01,015 --> 00:26:04,499
a particular kind
of bacteria thrives here

259
00:26:04,981 --> 00:26:06,944
and feeding
on the bacteria

260
00:26:06,984 --> 00:26:09,827
vast numbers of shrimps

261
00:26:20,316 --> 00:26:23,920
So beyond the
farthest reach of the sun's power

262
00:26:23,960 --> 00:26:27,002
A rich independent community exists

263
00:26:27,042 --> 00:26:31,886
That draws all its energy directly from the earth's molten core

264
00:26:47,810 --> 00:26:49,691
On the other
side of the planet

265
00:26:49,731 --> 00:26:52,733
in the western
Pacific bordering Japan

266
00:26:54,215 --> 00:26:56,297
the dragon chimneys

267
00:26:56,337 --> 00:27:01,060
another series of
hot vents erupting in the darkness

268
00:27:08,266 --> 00:27:09,707
Here，more

269
00:27:09,747 --> 00:27:14,150
but different
bacteria thrive in a similar way

270
00:27:19,697 --> 00:27:20,777
And here too

271
00:27:20,817 --> 00:27:22,298
more crustaceans

272
00:27:22,338 --> 00:27:27,581
but quite different species from those around the hot vents in the Atlantic

273
00:27:33,929 --> 00:27:36,888
These are
squat lobsters

274
00:27:36,928 --> 00:27:38,647
clad in
furry armour

275
00:27:38,687 --> 00:27:40,247
jostling
with one another

276
00:27:40,287 --> 00:27:43,006
beside the jets
of superheated water

277
00:27:43,046 --> 00:27:44,486
for the
best places

278
00:27:44,526 --> 00:27:47,205
from which to
graze on bacteria

279
00:27:53,559 --> 00:27:55,281
These vents too

280
00:27:55,321 --> 00:27:57,043
like those in
the Atlantic

281
00:27:57,083 --> 00:27:59,325
are isolated oases

282
00:27:59,365 --> 00:28:01,167
So widely
separated

283
00:28:01,207 --> 00:28:04,090
that each
community is unique

284
00:28:14,997 --> 00:28:17,716
Cross to the
other side of the Pacific

285
00:28:17,756 --> 00:28:20,876
to the deep near
the Galapagos Islands

286
00:28:20,916 --> 00:28:22,916
And there are
yet other fissures

287
00:28:22,956 --> 00:28:25,396
venting
superheated water

288
00:28:31,355 --> 00:28:33,515
One and a
half miles down

289
00:28:33,555 --> 00:28:36,435
at a site
known as Nine North

290
00:28:36,475 --> 00:28:40,115
towering chimneys
support a spectacular display

291
00:28:40,155 --> 00:28:42,515
of giant
tube worms

292
00:28:48,760 --> 00:28:51,440
These vents give
off so much energy

293
00:28:51,480 --> 00:28:55,360
that some of the
worms reach three metres in length

294
00:28:55,920 --> 00:28:59,719
They're the fastest growing marine invertebrates known

295
00:29:04,959 --> 00:29:06,159
All told

296
00:29:06,199 --> 00:29:08,559
over 50
different species

297
00:29:08,599 --> 00:29:11,279
Have so far been
found living here

298
00:29:15,612 --> 00:29:18,891
The inhabitants
of these bustling communities

299
00:29:18,931 --> 00:29:20,851
may grow
and speed

300
00:29:20,931 --> 00:29:23,851
But their
existence can also be short

301
00:29:23,891 --> 00:29:27,251
For the vents do
not erupt indefinitely

302
00:29:27,811 --> 00:29:28,731
Suddenly

303
00:29:28,771 --> 00:29:29,931
unpredictably

304
00:29:29,971 --> 00:29:32,011
they may
become inactive

305
00:29:37,147 --> 00:29:40,474
Nine months have
passed at Nine North

306
00:29:41,037 --> 00:29:44,644
What were only
recently chimneys teeming with life

307
00:29:44,726 --> 00:29:46,611
Have turned into cold

308
00:29:46,651 --> 00:29:49,536
sterile
mineral monuments

309
00:29:53,334 --> 00:29:56,777
Some eddy deep in
the earth's crust

310
00:29:56,938 --> 00:30:00,021
diverted the
volcanic energy elsewhere

311
00:30:00,182 --> 00:30:04,746
And an entire
microworld was extinguished

312
00:30:13,197 --> 00:30:14,478
In places

313
00:30:14,518 --> 00:30:18,843
volcanoes have
erupted to build great submarine mountains

314
00:30:19,579 --> 00:30:24,060
There are thought
to be around 30，000 such volcanoes

315
00:30:24,420 --> 00:30:25,221
Some

316
00:30:25,261 --> 00:30:27,101
measured from
the sea floor

317
00:30:27,141 --> 00:30:29,382
are taller
than Everest

318
00:30:35,625 --> 00:30:40,786
Sheer cliffs
soaring to drowned volcanic peaks

319
00:30:52,872 --> 00:30:56,314
Powerful currents
sweep up the mountain's flanks

320
00:30:56,354 --> 00:31:00,796
transporting nutrients from deep water towards the summits

321
00:31:04,077 --> 00:31:07,079
The hard rock
provides excellent anchorage

322
00:31:07,119 --> 00:31:09,560
for communities
of great variety

323
00:31:09,600 --> 00:31:11,761
and stunning colour

324
00:31:13,935 --> 00:31:15,096
Soft corals

325
00:31:15,136 --> 00:31:16,737
several
metres across

326
00:31:16,777 --> 00:31:19,259
collect the
marine snow

327
00:31:19,299 --> 00:31:21,340
as it drifts past

328
00:31:24,062 --> 00:31:27,704
Whip corals
stretch out into the current

329
00:31:30,387 --> 00:31:33,549
Giant sponges
filter nourishment

330
00:31:33,589 --> 00:31:35,310
from the
cold water

331
00:31:50,148 --> 00:31:53,672
A richly varied
community flourishes here

332
00:31:53,712 --> 00:31:56,715
sustained by the
nutrients and detritus

333
00:31:56,755 --> 00:32:00,358
in the icy currents that flow around the peak

334
00:32:05,691 --> 00:32:09,933
Yet it is all
blossoming on an extinct volcano

335
00:32:09,974 --> 00:32:12,935
a mile below the
reach of the sun

336
00:32:39,006 --> 00:32:40,366
Anoatetus

337
00:32:40,566 --> 00:32:44,126
It spends its
days hiding 400 metres down

338
00:32:44,166 --> 00:32:45,726
But as night falls

339
00:32:45,766 --> 00:32:49,286
it ascends up to
the reefs to look for food

340
00:32:55,225 --> 00:32:59,831
Its graceful shell contains gasfilled flotation chambers

341
00:32:59,871 --> 00:33:01,913
that
control its depth

342
00:33:04,957 --> 00:33:09,362
It's powered by a
jet of water squirting from a siphon

343
00:33:11,846 --> 00:33:13,768
But it
travels shell first

344
00:33:13,808 --> 00:33:17,332
so it can't see
exactly where it's going

345
00:33:22,854 --> 00:33:26,578
Its nearest living relatives are squid and octopus

346
00:33:26,618 --> 00:33:28,821
which over
evolutionary time

347
00:33:28,861 --> 00:33:31,423
have both
lost their shells

348
00:33:31,664 --> 00:33:36,309
And the octopus has become one of the nautilus's major predators

349
00:33:36,870 --> 00:33:39,593
It's a
master of disguise

350
00:33:46,121 --> 00:33:49,325
The nautilus
keeps well clear of them

351
00:33:51,902 --> 00:33:56,545
Its small tentacles carry highly developed chemical sensors

352
00:33:56,585 --> 00:34:00,947
which can detect
traces of both predators and prey

353
00:34:06,951 --> 00:34:11,394
It uses its water
jet to dig in the sand

354
00:34:16,551 --> 00:34:19,326
Because it devotes so little energy to swimming

355
00:34:19,366 --> 00:34:22,338
It only needs a
meal once a month

356
00:34:27,930 --> 00:34:29,290
Got something

357
00:34:30,332 --> 00:34:31,533
And just as well

358
00:34:31,573 --> 00:34:32,894
dawn is
approaching

359
00:34:32,934 --> 00:34:37,057
and it has to
puff its way back to deeper waters

360
00:35:00,015 --> 00:35:01,056
30 miles away

361
00:35:01,096 --> 00:35:05,339
shoals of squid
are jetting upwards towards the surface

362
00:35:07,101 --> 00:35:07,781
By night

363
00:35:07,821 --> 00:35:10,343
they seek small
fish among the plankton

364
00:35:10,383 --> 00:35:11,944
but they'
re cautious

365
00:35:20,150 --> 00:35:22,032
Pacific
spotted dolphin

366
00:35:26,710 --> 00:35:29,110
They're guided
by their sonar

367
00:35:34,189 --> 00:35:35,029
The dolphins

368
00:35:35,069 --> 00:35:36,149
as so often

369
00:35:36,189 --> 00:35:37,989
are working as a team

370
00:35:38,029 --> 00:35:39,949
Synchronising
their attacks

371
00:35:39,989 --> 00:35:41,949
to confuse
their prey

372
00:36:01,163 --> 00:36:02,683
As dawn approaches

373
00:36:02,723 --> 00:36:05,363
squid and
fish and plankton

374
00:36:05,403 --> 00:36:06,883
retreat downwards

375
00:36:06,923 --> 00:36:09,122
to shelter in
the darkness

376
00:36:36,328 --> 00:36:38,970
Some of these
isolated volcanoes

377
00:36:39,010 --> 00:36:42,813
rise as much as 9
000 metres from the sea floor

378
00:36:42,853 --> 00:36:45,174
reaching close
to the surface

379
00:36:49,339 --> 00:36:53,262
Around these
peaks invigorated by daily sunshine

380
00:36:53,302 --> 00:36:57,905
marine life
flourishes in spectacular abundance

381
00:37:07,477 --> 00:37:09,519
Fish crowd here

382
00:37:09,559 --> 00:37:13,244
because the volcano forces nutrients to the surface

383
00:37:13,284 --> 00:37:16,166
encouraging the
plankton to bloom

384
00:37:35,614 --> 00:37:37,377
An oceanic wanderer

385
00:37:37,417 --> 00:37:38,738
a mola mola

386
00:37:38,778 --> 00:37:41,862
stops by to be
cleaned by reef fish

387
00:37:41,902 --> 00:37:43,663
at the sea
mount edge

388
00:37:52,043 --> 00:37:57,363
Butterfly fish
pluck stringlike parasites from its flanks

389
00:38:04,321 --> 00:38:09,040
The huge fish lives on jellyfish over a thousand metres down

390
00:38:09,080 --> 00:38:12,160
where the water
is twenty degrees colder

391
00:38:13,310 --> 00:38:13,910
So

392
00:38:13,950 --> 00:38:15,991
a brush up
near the surface

393
00:38:16,031 --> 00:38:17,872
allows it
to warm up

394
00:38:17,912 --> 00:38:21,033
before making
more deep water forays

395
00:38:29,236 --> 00:38:31,996
The summit of
this volcanic mountain

396
00:38:32,036 --> 00:38:35,197
rises above the
surface of the sea

397
00:38:36,157 --> 00:38:39,758
It's Ascension Island

398
00:38:39,798 --> 00:38:41,878
800 miles from
any other land

399
00:38:42,238 --> 00:38:46,919
A welcome vital
haven for long distance travellers

400
00:38:52,068 --> 00:38:56,589
Frigate birds
spend months continuously airborne at sea

401
00:38:56,909 --> 00:38:58,389
But at
nesting time

402
00:38:58,429 --> 00:39:02,109
they come to a
scission from all over the ocean

403
00:39:06,870 --> 00:39:11,071
The island's
barren slopes of volcanic ash and lava

404
00:39:11,111 --> 00:39:14,911
Might seem to
offer perfectly good sites for a nest

405
00:39:16,986 --> 00:39:21,391
But the frigates
choose an even more isolated site

406
00:39:23,313 --> 00:39:25,035
Bosunbird Island

407
00:39:25,075 --> 00:39:28,959
a lonely pillar
just off Ascension's coast

408
00:39:37,671 --> 00:39:40,716
Frigates are the
world's lightest bird

409
00:39:40,756 --> 00:39:42,599
relative to
their wingspan

410
00:39:42,639 --> 00:39:45,123
And they can soar
for weeks on end

411
00:39:45,163 --> 00:39:47,045
with
minimal effort

412
00:39:51,413 --> 00:39:54,017
They seem much
more at home in the skies

413
00:39:54,057 --> 00:39:56,820
Than in a
crowded colony on land

414
00:39:56,901 --> 00:39:59,024
But nest they must

415
00:40:01,357 --> 00:40:06,286
They come from all over the Atlantic to this their only colony

416
00:40:09,774 --> 00:40:11,817
There are
boobies here too

417
00:40:17,247 --> 00:40:18,807
To raise
their young

418
00:40:18,847 --> 00:40:23,489
sea birds worldwide seek such remote islands

419
00:40:34,734 --> 00:40:38,335
Swimmers also
come to Ascension to breed

420
00:40:40,016 --> 00:40:43,817
A female green
turtle approaches the coast

421
00:40:46,953 --> 00:40:50,112
She's not eaten
once in two months

422
00:40:51,952 --> 00:40:56,271
She may have travelled 1000 miles from her feeding grounds

423
00:40:56,311 --> 00:40:59,310
The greatest
journey of her kind

424
00:41:06,872 --> 00:41:08,713
Many others
are here too

425
00:41:08,753 --> 00:41:10,914
resting on the
sandy sea floor

426
00:41:10,954 --> 00:41:12,996
awaiting the
darkness of night

427
00:41:13,036 --> 00:41:15,957
when it'll be
safer to visit the beaches

428
00:41:22,722 --> 00:41:26,844
Eggs that were laid a few weeks ago at the start of the season

429
00:41:26,884 --> 00:41:28,565
are beginning to hatch

430
00:41:41,927 --> 00:41:44,647
Most hatchings
happen at night

431
00:41:45,288 --> 00:41:45,808
Now

432
00:41:45,848 --> 00:41:47,088
in the
light of day

433
00:41:47,128 --> 00:41:49,928
the young are
extremely vulnerable

434
00:41:56,289 --> 00:41:59,609
They must get to
the sea as soon as possible

435
00:42:01,968 --> 00:42:05,328
But their trials
have only just begun

436
00:42:18,686 --> 00:42:22,725
Many will drown
in the pounding waves

437
00:42:48,065 --> 00:42:50,066
During the
next 20 years

438
00:42:50,106 --> 00:42:53,709
the vast majority
will inevitably die

439
00:42:53,869 --> 00:42:56,551
But those that
survive will eventually

440
00:42:56,591 --> 00:42:58,553
as their mothers
did before them

441
00:42:58,593 --> 00:43:00,875
return to the
very same beach

442
00:43:00,915 --> 00:43:02,515
where they
were hatched

443
00:43:04,958 --> 00:43:06,799
How they find
their way back

444
00:43:06,839 --> 00:43:09,681
across thousands
of miles of open ocean

445
00:43:09,721 --> 00:43:12,042
we still
have no idea

446
00:43:21,033 --> 00:43:23,194
A frigate saw us

447
00:43:24,918 --> 00:43:27,921
Somewhere beneath
the surface below

448
00:43:27,961 --> 00:43:30,524
there is the
food it must have

449
00:43:31,366 --> 00:43:32,527
But where？

450
00:43:35,051 --> 00:43:37,334
Those that fly
above the ocean

451
00:43:37,374 --> 00:43:40,897
must be able to
read the signs of fresh supplies

452
00:43:40,978 --> 00:43:42,219
or perish

453
00:43:45,790 --> 00:43:48,470
100 miles from
the Mexican coast

454
00:43:48,510 --> 00:43:51,350
and keen eyes
have spotted movement

455
00:43:56,551 --> 00:43:57,631
Sailfish

456
00:43:57,671 --> 00:43:59,111
three metres long

457
00:43:59,151 --> 00:44:01,791
are closing
in on prey

458
00:44:07,979 --> 00:44:12,665
They will only
use just enough energy to make their kill

459
00:44:13,266 --> 00:44:15,949
Never wasting
a fin stroke

460
00:44:20,557 --> 00:44:25,283
Nearly a hundred sailfish have surrounded a single school of baitfish

461
00:44:25,323 --> 00:44:29,809
It's very rare to
see so many of these hunters in one place

462
00:44:33,026 --> 00:44:34,390
To herd their prey

463
00:44:34,430 --> 00:44:38,117
the predators
raise their huge dorsal fins

464
00:44:56,591 --> 00:44:59,154
A mistimed
strike by one selfish

465
00:44:59,194 --> 00:45:01,156
could fatally
damage another

466
00:45:01,196 --> 00:45:03,959
But each continually changes its colour

467
00:45:03,999 --> 00:45:06,762
From blue to
stripes to black

468
00:45:06,922 --> 00:45:10,166
That warns its
companions of its intentions

469
00:45:10,206 --> 00:45:12,608
And also
confuses the prey

470
00:45:15,732 --> 00:45:18,215
As the shoal is
driven nearer the surface

471
00:45:18,255 --> 00:45:21,497
It comes within
the range of the seabirds

472
00:45:36,615 --> 00:45:38,655
Out here in
the open ocean

473
00:45:38,695 --> 00:45:42,215
There is nowhere
for the baitfish to hide

474
00:46:26,263 --> 00:46:29,903
Sailfish live a
high octane life

475
00:46:30,543 --> 00:46:31,663
To survive

476
00:46:31,703 --> 00:46:34,064
they must
find prey daily

477
00:46:34,784 --> 00:46:39,144
So their entire
existence will be spent on the move

478
00:46:50,869 --> 00:46:55,630
Over 90% of the
living space for life on our planet

479
00:46:55,670 --> 00:46:57,670
is in the oceans

480
00:46:58,910 --> 00:47:02,591
Home to the
biggest animal that exists

481
00:47:02,751 --> 00:47:05,072
or has
ever existed

482
00:47:06,792 --> 00:47:08,833
The blue whale

483
00:47:17,048 --> 00:47:20,571
Some weigh
nearly 200 tons

484
00:47:20,732 --> 00:47:24,374
twice the size of
the largest dinosaur

485
00:47:29,299 --> 00:47:31,461
Despite
their great size

486
00:47:31,501 --> 00:47:33,343
we still
have little idea

487
00:47:33,383 --> 00:47:36,505
of where they
travel in the vast oceans

488
00:47:36,666 --> 00:47:40,189
And none at all
of where they go to breed

489
00:48:10,523 --> 00:48:15,290
The largest animal on Earth feeds almost exclusively

490
00:48:15,330 --> 00:48:17,371
on one of
the smallest

491
00:48:17,613 --> 00:48:18,613
krill

492
00:48:18,734 --> 00:48:21,016
shrimplike
crustaceans

493
00:48:22,860 --> 00:48:25,063
They take many
tonnes of water

494
00:48:25,103 --> 00:48:28,827
into their
ballooning throats in a single gulp

495
00:48:28,867 --> 00:48:31,831
and sieve out
what it contains

496
00:48:38,987 --> 00:48:40,348
Every day

497
00:48:40,549 --> 00:48:45,233
each one swallows
some four million krill

498
00:48:48,077 --> 00:48:50,760
Such gargantuan harvests

499
00:48:50,800 --> 00:48:54,764
depend on the
continuing fertility of the oceans

500
00:48:57,648 --> 00:49:02,374
But global changes now threaten the great blooms of plankton

501
00:49:02,414 --> 00:49:04,575
on which the
whales depend

502
00:49:07,242 --> 00:49:08,203
Once

503
00:49:08,285 --> 00:49:10,167
and not
so long ago

504
00:49:12,133 --> 00:49:14,697
300，000 blue
whales roamed the oceans

505
00:49:15,580 --> 00:49:16,262
Now

506
00:49:16,302 --> 00:49:19,908
less than 3% of
that number remains

507
00:49:24,700 --> 00:49:28,343
Our planet is
still full of wonders

508
00:49:28,704 --> 00:49:30,385
As we explore them

509
00:49:30,425 --> 00:49:33,868
so we gain not
only understanding

510
00:49:33,908 --> 00:49:35,109
but power

511
00:49:37,151 --> 00:49:41,955
It's not just the
future of the whale that today lies in our hands

512
00:49:42,356 --> 00:49:45,158
It's the survival
of the natural world

513
00:49:45,198 --> 00:49:48,481
In all parts of
the living planet

514
00:49:50,071 --> 00:49:51,951
We can now destroy

515
00:49:52,232 --> 00:49:53,913
or we can cherish

516
00:49:57,236 --> 00:50:00,197
The choice is ours

